arƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Mq7bh0BV48J7NbE2v62j4C-SXW2sEW_Yz48lb-cwIXUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;AzWzCO46zLL-f_eznbrVXqc4p-0DC7fkruVihBePxg8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;L0izVuQh23MyGbcZ5E7CsDdpufMZHFLi-zC14tSB-HQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;daXeROfegbe9nTd4G03wBvDGAZEG80rg3QAyym1kX-Yƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;zWbR8PHq41SdKNqemr06Y5tSkBf0ygZJjTunRICx5Q0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$7149fe40-6ecb-4629-8f99-fb0946766bafƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Rtl1g_2icigvb2TZonJ4Ey5Wt7c7ODu-KKVsWrfxi5Qƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky.TITLE: Economics and the Biophysical Limits to Growth: A New Collection Frames the Discipline's Biggest-ever Question
DESCRIPTION: A new economic collection edited by Fullbrook and Morgan asks 23 voices to confront the biophysical limits that growth-era models have long sidestepped.
SUMMARY: A collection edited by Edward Fullbrook and Jamie Morgan gathers 23 responses to economics' biggest-ever question: whether the discipline can face the biophysical limits its models have long ignored. The book's framing rests on a claim from natural science â€” the global economy caused the climate crisis, and the crisis now threatens the economy's continued existence. That feedback loop is in an accelerating phase.
TWEET: A new collection asks 23 economists to answer what growth-era models have deferred: whether the economy can survive its own biophysical limits ğŸŒ
TAGS: limits to growth, ecological economics, biophysical limits, climate economics, degrowth
BODY:

A new collection edited by Edward Fullbrook and Jamie Morgan, [23 Responses to Economics' Biggest-ever Question](https://rwer.wordpress.com/2026/07/14/23-responses-to-economics-biggest-ever-question/), puts the most consequential unresolved question in economic thought on the table: whether an economy organized around growth can continue within the biophysical limits of a finite planet â€” and what it means for the discipline if it cannot. Subtitled "Economics and the Biophysical Limits to Economic Growth," the volume's framing rests on a claim made by natural science: the global economy caused the climate crisis, and the crisis, now in an accelerating phase, threatens the economy's own existence. For a discipline that has "loosely guided the global economy's national economies" for over a century, that is not a refinement. It is a structural challenge.

## A Century of Guidance, a Question Left Unasked

Twenty-three separate responses to a single framing question, assembled in one volume, signals that Fullbrook and Morgan understand this not as a debate with a settled answer but as a live rupture in the discipline's foundations. That it is framed as the biggest-ever question economics has faced, and that it generates this density of differentiated response, reflects a shift in urgency â€” one driven by the crisis entering its accelerating phase.

The collection's central claim, grounded in natural science rather than economic theory, is that the relationship between growth and its biophysical consequences has reversed direction: growth caused the crisis; the crisis now constrains growth. What has changed is the insistence that economics, as the discipline that has guided the global economy for over a century, must now account for what natural science has established â€” rather than defer it to another field.

## The Policy Architecture Already Built on a Bet

The difficulty is that major institutional decisions have not waited for this reckoning. Green growth â€” the premise that economic expansion can be decoupled from resource use and made consistent with ecological stability â€” is not a fringe hypothesis. [Green growth](https://unevenearth.org/2022/10/green-growth/) by Winne van Woerden for Uneven Earth documents it as the governing assumption embedded in the European Green Deal, the Paris Agreement, IPCC reports, and the US Inflation Reduction Act, promoted by leading multilateral organizations and treated as common sense across national and international policymaking.

The institutional bet embedded in those frameworks is that the answer to the collection's central question is yes â€” growth can continue, cleanly enough, within biophysical limits. If the 23 responses suggest otherwise, the implications don't settle in academic journals. They land in every policy instrument that has already staked its architecture on decoupling working at the required scale.

## Growth Mania Beyond the Material Economy

The logic the collection scrutinizes is not confined to resource extraction. [Growth mania is fueling dangerous advances in AI â€“ and its existential risks](https://www.resilience.org/stories/2026-07-09/growth-mania-is-fueling-dangerous-advances-in-ai-and-its-existential-risks/), published in Resilience, frames AI development as "the apex of growth mania" â€” decisions made by a small number of tech elites without the consent or interests of the broader population in mind. The pattern is structurally similar to the one the collection examines: growth imperatives generating consequences externalized onto those who didn't drive the growth, with no internal mechanism registering accumulating cost until the feedback becomes impossible to absorb.

Whether the same discipline that missed this dynamic in the material economy is equipped to track it in digital infrastructure is not a question the current volume takes on. But the logic of compounding externalities the collection foregrounds runs through both domains.

## What 23 Responses Open

The collection resolves nothing, and it doesn't claim to. What the format makes visible is that the question is now being engaged at scale within mainstream economics â€” that biophysical limits have generated enough serious response to constitute a volume of 23 differentiated voices, not a pamphlet from the ecological economics margin.

That shift might matter less for what economics does next than for what it becomes harder to ignore. Every growth projection, every policy framework, every national economic plan runs on assumptions â€” stated or not â€” about what physical systems can sustain. The collection marks the moment when those assumptions became the question, not the premise.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;_Pvf0m_kmBjP7oM4QMa9M44MOZdP1A4-xbprvXaUmzAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;dW3TZAZEMINkDF9nEEunreULVIlyPv6XmS3gAfi9i88ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;NPV21SW4ycNEGZflE2YSeLNmdjk7GVbQ_Iknkf2MnGUƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ceBQ9kFAbGvA9UOdVoFpSkzjUJWFQDl5wVbGNHUzQGgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;QUfkzivz-JY_JkCdfet0dlJLldxA8Xcjed4emJW1Ploƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;_k4rd3zhPEGOLsdo62dtPEkbySuNfsEZT9KkUdQAI_cƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkg272.779ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;MQqNaveDMJRxpWLYuItMr27PAmwx_x9-f-w4q0cT5S8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;n5gRVHZ67in1eHy20PkapEyyJ7sqvZpZ5CiRpkvDc6kƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;6o143XL6TdCtfEKEU1ZI3keGdfE2_1jEpdFKCqa4fSoƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;j9NVTvtuYnw3EfitRn--z9xLUNYBgzf_meN5m_pHIsoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒ